# Anchor-to-anchor DS-TWR bench app.

mainmenu "openaliro anchor"

choice
	prompt "Anchor role"
	default ANCHOR_ROLE_INITIATOR
	help
	  Which end of the DS-TWR round this board is. The pair needs exactly one
	  of each; two initiators never hear a POLL and two responders never send
	  one, and both failures present as a silent link.

config ANCHOR_ROLE_INITIATOR
	bool "Initiator (sends POLL, sets the cadence)"

config ANCHOR_ROLE_RESPONDER
	bool "Responder (replies, computes the distance)"

endchoice

config ANCHOR_REPLY_DELAY_US
	int "Delayed-TX reply delay (us)"
	default 3000
	range 500 20000
	help
	  How long after a received frame's RMARKER the reply is scheduled. This
	  is the number to raise first if `late` is ever non-zero in the ANCHOR
	  report line, because a non-zero `late` means the chip found the
	  programmed instant already past when it looked.

	  3000 is not arbitrary. The engine's own arm-deadline history
	  (modules/woz_uwb/src/ccc/ccc_shim_rx.c, CCC_RX_POLL_LEAD) records that a
	  +1676 us deadline did NOT clear a settled dispatch latency of
	  1433-1616 us, and that moving it to +1836 us turned 60-240 us of margin
	  into 220-400 us. 3000 us leaves roughly 1.2 ms over the worst dispatch
	  that history measured, on a link whose cadence nobody else negotiates.

	  Lower it only with the `late` counter as evidence, never to make a
	  number look better.

config ANCHOR_ROUND_PERIOD_MS
	int "Initiator round period (ms)"
	default 100
	range 20 10000
	help
	  Gap between POLLs. 100 ms is 10 Hz, which is the stage-A range-rate pass
	  criterion. The responder has no equivalent setting: it waits, so the
	  initiator alone sets the cadence for the pair.

	  Deliberately NOT `depends on ANCHOR_ROLE_INITIATOR`. main() selects the
	  role with IS_ENABLED(), which compiles both branches and discards one at
	  link time, so the symbol has to exist in a responder build too. A
	  `depends on` here builds the initiator and breaks the responder, which is
	  a confusing way to learn how IS_ENABLED differs from #ifdef.

config ANCHOR_ANT_DLY_DTU
	int "Lumped antenna-delay calibration (DTU)"
	default 4092
	help
	  Subtracted from the computed time-of-flight before it becomes a
	  distance. Zero means uncalibrated; 4092 is the measured value for the
	  DWM3001CDK + nRF5340DK/DWM3000EVB pair this repository ranges with, and
	  it is only meaningful with CONFIG_ANCHOR_TX_512_ALIGN on. Nothing here
	  programs the DW3000's own TX_ANTD / CIA_CONF registers.

	  CALIBRATE THE ALIGNMENT BEFORE THE DELAY, NEVER THE OTHER WAY. Before
	  the 512 DTU alignment the range was bimodal, 817 mm and 1120 mm at a
	  true 1000, so any constant fitted then was fitted to a mixture and
	  described neither mode. With the alignment on, 2,229 samples read a mean
	  of 1003.3 mm and a median of 1004.0 -- a +3.3 mm residual. That residual
	  is statistically real, its standard error being 0.4 mm, and physically
	  irrelevant: it is 0.7 DTU, well under what a tape measure resolves.
	  Fitting it would be calibrating to the ruler.

	  Expect the first ~370 samples to read about 9 mm short of the rest. The
	  run above opens at 996.4 mm and settles at ~1005 for its remaining five
	  sixths, so a two-minute calibration will land low. Give it five.

	  Applied in the app rather than pushed into those registers on purpose.
	  A single distance measurement cannot separate the TX half from the RX
	  half, so writing two registers would be inventing a split the
	  measurement does not contain; one lumped constant in one visible place
	  is the honest shape.

	  IT IS A PER-PAIR CONSTANT, NOT A PER-BOARD ONE. Solving it at a known
	  distance with two boards absorbs both ends at once. That is exactly
	  right for a fixed anchor pair and wrong the moment a third board joins,
	  which will need per-board constants and a different procedure.

	  THE EXPECTED MAGNITUDE PRINTED HERE WAS WRONG AND WOULD HAVE REJECTED
	  THE CORRECT ANSWER. It said to expect a value near 16,000 and to treat
	  anything outside 8,000-32,000 as a fault to diagnose. The measured value
	  is 4,092, which that band calls broken. ~16,000 DTU is the magnitude of
	  the DW3000's own antenna-delay REGISTERS; this constant is a lumped
	  correction applied to the one-way time-of-flight instead, and there is
	  no reason for the two to be the same number. Trust a calibration against
	  a tape measure over any figure quoted here, including this one.

config ANCHOR_MEDIAN_N
	int "Median window for the published range (samples, must be odd)"
	default 1
	range 1 15
	help
	  DEFAULT 1, WHICH MEANS OFF. The filter is kept because the measurement
	  that retired it is worth not repeating, not because it is useful.

	  It was built for a diagnosis that turned out to be wrong. Stage A's
	  first run saw eight stationary samples at a tape-measured 1.000 m read
	  929, 975, 905, 929, 910, 619, 872, 600 mm, and read that as a sporadic
	  minority mode a median could outvote. Seventy samples later the false
	  mode was 37%, not 25%, and the median removed almost none of it: mean
	  absolute error 149.1 mm raw against 129.1 mm filtered. A per-tap
	  correction table did far better at 31.5 mm, and was also wrong, because
	  both were treating a symptom.

	  THE REAL DEFECT WAS THE DELAYED-TX TIMESTAMP. t5err, once it reported a
	  minimum beside its maximum, read 16149..16405 -- a spread of exactly 256
	  DTU in the difference between the predicted FINAL RMARKER and the one
	  the chip produced. Through DS-TWR that is 64 DTU of time-of-flight and
	  so 300.3 mm, against a measured 302 mm between the two range modes.
	  CONFIG_ANCHOR_TX_512_ALIGN fixes it, and with it on the range is
	  unimodal at 19.6 mm sd against 151.9 mm before, with 99.4% of 2,229
	  samples within 60 mm of the median and none outside 924-1083. There is
	  nothing left for a median to do.

	  WHAT TO TAKE FROM THIS, if a future run looks bimodal again: a maximum
	  cannot distinguish a constant from a spread, and the whole diagnosis
	  turned on adding a minimum next to one. Two hypotheses were wrong first
	  -- a sporadic half-chip slip, then first-path tap selection -- and both
	  produced plausible correlations. The first-path index correlated with
	  the range modes at r = -0.768, which looked like a mechanism and was a
	  coincidence of both being functions of t4's fine timing; after the fix
	  that correlation is +0.056 over 2,229 samples.

	  N MUST BE ODD, enforced by a BUILD_ASSERT in anchor_twr.c. An even
	  window has to combine its two middle samples, and against a bimodal
	  population those two can be one from each mode, so their average falls
	  in the gap between the modes -- the one value the measurement never
	  takes. Costs N * 4 B of .bss and the same again of stack.

config ANCHOR_LOG_EACH_RANGE
	bool "Log every accepted range with its first-path index"
	default y
	help
	  Print one line per accepted range: the published distance, the raw
	  distance before the median, and ipatovFpIndex from the FINAL reception
	  that produced it.

	  THIS EXISTS TO TEST ONE THING. The 1 m bench run measured a bimodal
	  range -- 817.2 mm at 62.9% and 1119.5 mm at 37.1%, separated by 302 mm.
	  One DW3000 accumulator tap is 1 / 998.4 MHz = 1.0016 ns = 300.4 mm, so
	  if the leading edge is latching on the adjacent tap the two modes must
	  differ by exactly one tap in ipatovFpIndex. That is a sharp prediction
	  and this is the cheapest way to check it.

	  WHY PER-RANGE AND NOT THE PERIODIC LINE. The periodic report samples
	  once per 20 s while the responder completes ~4.75 ranges per second, so
	  a 20-minute run yielded 70 observations out of 7,208 ranges. Estimating
	  a mode fraction from 1% of the population wastes the run.

	  main.c warns that a per-round line is a print every few tens of
	  milliseconds that the ranging path cannot absorb. That is the
	  INITIATOR's cadence. This logs only on the RESPONDER, only for ranges
	  that passed the plausibility check, at ~210 ms apart, and after the
	  round has completed -- nothing is armed and no deadline is pending.
	  Turn it off for any run that is measuring timing rather than geometry.

config ANCHOR_TX_512_ALIGN
	bool "Align delayed transmits to 512 DTU"
	default y
	help
	  Clear the low bit of the delayed-TX register value, so every scheduled
	  transmit lands on a 512 DTU boundary instead of a 256 DTU one.

	  WHY. The 1 m bench run measured t5err=16149..16405 -- the difference
	  between the predicted FINAL RMARKER and the one the chip reported spans
	  exactly 256 DTU. Through DS-TWR that is 64 DTU of time-of-flight and so
	  300.3 mm, against a measured 302 mm between the two range modes. The
	  bimodal range is this, not a first-path detection failure.

	  A two-valued 256 DTU error is the signature of hardware that ignores the
	  low nine bits of system time while the code predicts to eight: the true
	  instant is then ((dx & ~1) << 8), so the prediction is exact when dx is
	  even and 256 DTU early when it is odd. Forcing dx even makes it exact.

	  Costs 0 or 256 DTU off the reply delay, which is 4 ns against 3000 us.

	  n restores the previous behaviour, which is what every measurement
	  before this used. Keep it reachable: if the range stays bimodal with
	  this on, the theory is wrong and the comparison is the evidence.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
